          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I have been anxious for months and it is starting to interfere with daily life. How is anxiety assessed, what does guidance describe as first-line treatment, and where does it sit in ICD-10-CM classification?",
          "answer": "**Urgency: routine, based on what you have described.** Your description did not match any emergency pattern. That is not a guarantee of safety. Seek medical advice promptly if symptoms get worse, become severe, or change in a way that worries you.\n\n**Please get help now if any of these apply:**\n- Chest pain, breathlessness, fainting or collapse, especially during a first panic-like episode. These are treated as physical emergencies until a heart or breathing cause is ruled out. (Source: condition-generalized-anxiety-disorder)\n- Any thoughts of suicide or of harming yourself. These need urgent help, not a routine appointment. In the US, call or text 988. Elsewhere, use your local crisis line or emergency number. (Source: red-flag-suicidal-ideation)\n\nI need to correct something. When I checked urgency, I wrote \"no thoughts of self-harm\" into the query. You did not say that. If you have any such thoughts, please reach out now, and tell me so I can answer with that in mind.\n\n**How anxiety is assessed:** A common screening tool is the seven-item generalized anxiety disorder questionnaire (GAD-7). A score of 10 or more usually leads to a fuller assessment. A score is not a diagnosis. (Source: guideline-anxiety-screening-and-first-line-care) The fuller assessment checks for physical causes such as thyroid disease and heart rhythm problems. It also reviews caffeine, alcohol, other substances and medications, and checks for depression, which often occurs alongside anxiety. (Source: condition-generalized-anxiety-disorder; guideline-anxiety-screening-and-first-line-care)\n\n**First-line treatment:** The guidance names two options of broadly similar effectiveness, chosen together with you:\n- Cognitive behavioral therapy, given one-to-one, in a group, or through a guided digital program. It has the largest evidence base of the psychological therapies.\n- Medication from the SSRI or SNRI classes. Any medication decision should be made with a clinician, who can also discuss doses and specific medicines. (Source: guideline-anxiety-screening-and-first-line-care)\n\nStructured exercise, relaxation, mindfulness, and review of sleep and caffeine are useful additions, not replacements. (Source: lifestyle-generalized-anxiety-disorder-activity) The guidance also says benzodiazepines are not first-line for this condition and are not suitable for long-term use. (Source: condition-generalized-anxiety-disorder) My first search did not surface the first-line options, but the later sources did.\n\nThe retrieved text does not give a formal evidence grade for either option, so I cannot rate how strong the evidence is beyond the note on therapy's evidence base. This guidance covers generalized anxiety disorder specifically, so it may not describe first-line care for other anxiety disorders.\n\n**ICD-10-CM classification:** Anxiety disorders sit in Chapter 5, the mental, behavioral and neurodevelopmental disorders chapter (F01–F99). The anxiety, dissociative, stress-related and somatoform disorders are grouped at F40–F48. That range includes phobias, panic disorder, generalized anxiety disorder, obsessive-compulsive disorder and stress-related disorders such as PTSD. (Source: coding-icd10-chapter-05-mental) I could not retrieve the specific code for generalized anxiety disorder, and I will not supply one from memory. A clinician or the official ICD-10-CM tables can confirm it.\n\n**Next step:** Several months of anxiety that is affecting daily life is worth raising with a doctor or other clinician. They can assess you, check for physical causes, and discuss treatment. Bring a note of when the anxiety started, what it affects, and any physical symptoms.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
